{
  "chalk": 1,
  "name": "02-normal-forms.chalk",
  "cells": [
    {
      "src": "Equivalence and normal forms",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!goal]\n> Write down a formula for any truth table, then reach the same normal forms from a formula by the laws of logic, one law a step.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Lesson 1 went from a formula to its table. Here is a table with no formula:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "$$\\begin{array}{ccc|c} p & q & r & \\ ? \\\\ \\hline T & T & T & F \\\\ T & T & F & T \\\\ T & F & T & T \\\\ T & F & F & F \\\\ F & T & T & F \\\\ F & T & F & F \\\\ F & F & T & F \\\\ F & F & F & T \\end{array}$$",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!try]\n> Find a formula with this table. Better: a method that works for any table.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "A formula for any table",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Take one true row, $p, q, r = T, T, F$. The formula $p \\land q \\land \\lnot r$ is true in that row and in no other: each literal pins down one variable. Write one such formula for each true row and join them with $\\lor$. The result is true exactly when one of them is, which is exactly in the true rows:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "truthtable((p ∧ q ∧ ¬r) ∨ (p ∧ ¬q ∧ r) ∨ (¬p ∧ ¬q ∧ ¬r))",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Nothing in that used this particular table. So every truth table has a formula, built from $\\lnot$, $\\land$ and $\\lor$ alone (a table with no true row gets $\\bot$).",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!definition] Literal, term, clause, DNF, CNF\n> A **literal** is a variable or its negation. A **term** is an $\\land$ of literals and a **clause** an $\\lor$ of them. A formula is in **disjunctive normal form** (DNF) when it is an $\\lor$ of terms, as the one just built is, and in **conjunctive normal form** (CNF) when it is an $\\land$ of clauses.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The same works upside down. A clause can be false in exactly one row: $\\lnot p \\lor \\lnot q \\lor \\lnot r$ is false only at $T, T, T$. One such clause for each false row, joined with $\\land$, rules out the false rows and nothing else: a CNF. So every formula is equivalent to one in DNF and to one in CNF.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!definition] Logical equivalence\n> $\\varphi \\equiv \\psi$ when $\\varphi$ and $\\psi$ have the same value under every assignment: the same truth table. Replacing a part of a formula by an equivalent one does not change the value of the whole.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Without the table",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Reading off rows costs a term per true row, and a formula in 20 variables has $2^{20} = 1048576$ rows. The laws of logic work on the formula instead. Each replaces a part by an equivalent part, and each is checked once and for all by a table of a few rows.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!theorem] The laws used below\n> $p \\to q \\equiv \\lnot p \\lor q$, $\\quad p \\leftrightarrow q \\equiv (p \\to q) \\land (q \\to p)$, $\\quad \\lnot\\lnot p \\equiv p$,\n>\n> De Morgan: $\\lnot(p \\land q) \\equiv \\lnot p \\lor \\lnot q$ and $\\lnot(p \\lor q) \\equiv \\lnot p \\land \\lnot q$,\n>\n> distribution: $p \\lor (q \\land r) \\equiv (p \\lor q) \\land (p \\lor r)$ and $p \\land (q \\lor r) \\equiv (p \\land q) \\lor (p \\land r)$.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The first is lesson 1's table of $\\to$ read as a formula: false only when $p$ is true and $q$ false. Each law is proved in Lean over the booleans, and the engine's normal forms are proved to keep the value of the formula (the dot next to each step).",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Negation normal form",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!definition] Negation normal form (NNF)\n> No $\\to$ or $\\leftrightarrow$, and $\\lnot$ only directly on variables. Get there by eliminating the arrows, then pushing each negation inward with De Morgan's laws until it reaches a variable, cancelling double negations on the way.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Step through it: say which law comes next before you press `▸ Next step`.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "nnf(¬(p ∧ (q ∨ ¬r)))",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null,
      "stepwise": 0
    },
    {
      "src": "nnf(¬(p → q))",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!mistake]\n> The natural guess is that $\\lnot$ just moves inside: $\\lnot(p \\land q) \\equiv \\lnot p \\land \\lnot q$. But \"not both\" is weaker than \"neither\". The engine finds the row where they differ:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "equiv(¬(p ∧ q), ¬p ∧ ¬q)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "At $p$ true and $q$ false, \"not both\" holds and \"neither\" does not. De Morgan's law is the repair: the $\\land$ turns into $\\lor$, since \"not both\" means \"at least one fails\".",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Conjunctive and disjunctive normal form",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "From NNF, distribute $\\lor$ over $\\land$ for CNF (or $\\land$ over $\\lor$ for DNF), as $a(b + c) = ab + ac$ in arithmetic, except that here each distributes over the other. A clause that contains both $p$ and $\\lnot p$ is always true and drops out; so does a term with both, which is always false.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "cnf(p ∨ (q ∧ r))",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "cnf(p ↔ q)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null,
      "stepwise": 0
    },
    {
      "src": "dnf(p ↔ q)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null,
      "stepwise": 0
    },
    {
      "src": "The DNF lists the two true rows of the table of $p \\leftrightarrow q$, both false or both true, reached without the table.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!note] Why CNF, and its cost\n> A CNF is a list of constraints that must all hold, each satisfied by any one of its literals. SAT solvers, which decide satisfiability for formulas with millions of variables, take their input in CNF. Distribution has a price, though: each further pair joined by $\\lor$ doubles the clauses.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "cnf((p ∧ q) ∨ (r ∧ s) ∨ (t ∧ u))",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Three pairs give $2^3 = 8$ clauses, and $n$ pairs $2^n$. Solvers avoid that by naming subformulas with new variables (the Tseitin encoding), which keeps satisfiability rather than equivalence.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Exercises",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Your answer must be in the form asked for and equivalent to the formula. Equivalence is decided by truth table, so a \"not yet\" is definite: the answer differs from the formula in some row.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "nnf(¬(p ∨ ¬q))",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Put $\\lnot(p \\lor \\lnot q)$ in negation normal form.",
      "hints": [
        "De Morgan: $\\lnot(a \\lor b) \\equiv \\lnot a \\land \\lnot b$.",
        "Then $\\lnot\\lnot q \\equiv q$."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "nnf(p → q)",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Write $p \\to q$ without $\\to$: in negation normal form.",
      "hints": [
        "$p \\to q$ fails only when $p$ is true and $q$ false."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "cnf((p ∧ q) ∨ r)",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Put $(p \\land q) \\lor r$ in conjunctive normal form.",
      "hints": [
        "Distribute $\\lor r$ over the $\\land$: each conjunct gets its own $\\lor r$."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "dnf(p ∧ (q ∨ r))",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Put $p \\land (q \\lor r)$ in disjunctive normal form.",
      "hints": [
        "Distribute $p \\land$ over the $\\lor$."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "cnf(¬(p ∧ q) ∧ (p ∨ q))",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Put $\\lnot(p \\land q) \\land (p \\lor q)$ in CNF. (It says \"exactly one of $p$, $q$\".)",
      "hints": [
        "Only the first part needs work: De Morgan."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "dnf((p ∧ q) ∨ (q ∧ r) ∨ (p ∧ r))",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "The **majority** of $p$, $q$, $r$ is true when at least two of them are. Write it in DNF, from its truth table or from the words.",
      "hints": [
        "Its true rows are $T, T, T$, then $T, T, F$, then $T, F, T$, then $F, T, T$: one term each.",
        "Or shorter: any two being true is enough, and a term need not mention the third variable."
      ],
      "hideQuestion": true,
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!summary]\n> Every truth table has a formula: the $\\lor$ of its true rows (a DNF), or the $\\land$ of clauses that rule out its false rows (a CNF). The laws of logic reach the same forms from a formula without its table: NNF pushes negations onto the variables, and distributing gives CNF or DNF.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Tables and laws decide formulas about $p$ and $q$. Neither can check a statement about every number, which has infinitely many rows. The next lesson builds proofs instead, step by step, and has Lean check them.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    }
  ],
  "scenes": []
}
